\chapter{Numerical Solver Architecture}

\section{Overview}

The STME-RS-A propulsion system employs a multi-tier numerical solver
architecture designed to handle stiff thermochemical kinetics, high-density
fluid dynamics, and geometric–plasma coupling. The solver stack is optimized
for parallel execution across 32+ cores using Intel Threading Building Blocks
(TBB) and C++20 coroutines.

\section{Solver Pipeline Structure}

The solver pipeline consists of three primary modules:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Stiff Navier–Stokes Solver}
    \item \textbf{Finite-Rate Chemistry Integrator}
    \item \textbf{Geometry–Plasma Coupling Module}
\end{enumerate}

Each module operates asynchronously under the Core Orchestrator, with
synchronization points defined by residual norm thresholds.

\section{Stiff Navier–Stokes Solver}

The fluid dynamics solver is based on the compressible Navier–Stokes
equations with stiff source terms:

\[
\frac{\partial \mathbf{U}}{\partial t}
+ \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{U})
= \mathbf{S}_{\text{stiff}}(\mathbf{U}),
\]

where:

\[
\mathbf{U} = [\rho, \rho \mathbf{v}, E]^{\mathsf{T}}.
\]

An implicit backward differentiation formula (BDF) is used for stability:

\[
\mathbf{U}^{(n+1)} - \Delta t \, \beta \, \mathbf{S}_{\text{stiff}}
\left( \mathbf{U}^{(n+1)} \right)
= \mathbf{U}^{(n)}.
\]

Parallel domain decomposition is applied across the chamber mesh.

\section{Finite-Rate Chemistry Solver}

The chemical kinetics module solves:

\[
\frac{dY_i}{dt} = \omega_i(T, P, Y_1, \ldots, Y_N),
\]

using an adaptive Runge–Kutta–Fehlberg (RKF45) integrator. Reaction stiffness
is mitigated through:

\begin{itemize}
    \item temperature-based step size control,
    \item Jacobian preconditioning,
    \item species clustering for reduced dimensionality.
\end{itemize}

The solver exchanges enthalpy and species mass fractions with the Navier–Stokes
module at each synchronization point.

\section{Geometry–Plasma Coupling Module}

The chamber geometry follows a gyroid–Schwarz minimal-surface hybrid. Plasma
interaction is modeled through a curvature-driven coupling coefficient:

\[
\Gamma = \frac{\partial \mathbf{U}}{\partial \kappa},
\]

where \( \kappa \) is the local geometric curvature.

Topological invariants are monitored:

\[
\mathcal{W} = 1, \qquad C = 1,
\]

ensuring stability across plasma boundary layers.

\section{Residual Norm Convergence}

Solver convergence is defined by:

\[
\mathcal{R} = \left\| \mathbf{U}^{(n+1)} - \mathbf{U}^{(n)} \right\|_2,
\]

with the following criteria:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Acceptable:} \( \mathcal{R} < 10^{-6} \)
    \item \textbf{Warning:} \( 10^{-6} \leq \mathcal{R} < 10^{-4} \)
    \item \textbf{Failure:} \( \mathcal{R} \geq 10^{-4} \)
\end{itemize}

Residual norms are streamed to the HDF5 ring buffer for real-time visualization.

\section{Parallel Execution Model}

Intel TBB manages:

\begin{itemize}
    \item task-based parallelism,
    \item solver pipeline scheduling,
    \item asynchronous hardware feedback integration.
\end{itemize}

C++20 coroutines allow non-blocking solver updates synchronized with telemetry
and hardware-in-the-loop signals.

\section{Summary}

The solver architecture integrates stiff fluid dynamics, chemical kinetics,
and geometric–plasma coupling into a unified, parallelized computational
framework. This chapter establishes the numerical foundation for all
simulation-driven components of the STME-RS-A propulsion system.